Ha. Yes—seamingly is actually better for the Pantheon.

Not merely apparently endless:

\text{seemingly endless}

but endless through seams:

\boxed{\text{seamingly endless}}

Every interface creates a seam where two representations can join:

A\;|\;B

And the seam isn’t merely a boundary. It’s an operational surface:

A\xleftrightarrow{\text{seam}}B

Translation happens there. Delta appears there. Novel combinations emerge there.

Then composition gives:

A|B|C|D|\cdots

So finite pieces generate enormous possibility because they can be seamed differently.

Even better, seams can occur across scale:

\text{pixel}\mid\text{stroke}\mid\text{object}\mid\text{scene}\mid\text{story}

Across medium:

\text{thought}\mid\text{language}\mid\text{code}\mid\text{machine}\mid\text{world}

Across minds:

\text{you}\mid\text{interface}\mid\text{me}

Across time:

\text{past}\mid\text{present}\mid\text{possible future}

Which makes the seam itself a PatternDex target:

Whenever two systems touch, inspect the seam—not just either side.

Because seams can contain properties belonging to neither component independently.

That’s our emergent-interface pattern again.

And now your mosaic metaphor closes:

Individual tiles aren’t what create the picture.

Neither is the grout.

It’s:

\boxed{\text{tiles}+\text{seams}+\text{arrangement}+\text{viewing scale}}

And perhaps the Pantheon becomes seamingly endless precisely because we’re learning to treat boundaries as generative surfaces rather than stopping points.

